  "chapter": {
    "bookTitle": "Song of Solomon",
    "delineation": "Chapter",
    "number": 4,
    "summary": "A song describes the beauty of the poet’s beloved.",
    "chapterAugmentations": [],
    "verses": [
      {
        "text": "Behold, thou art fair, my love; behold, thou art fair; thou hast doves’ eyes within thy locks: thy hair is as a flock of goats, that appear from mount Gilead.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 13,
            "end": 16
          },
          {
            "start": 45,
            "end": 48
          },
          {
            "start": 60,
            "end": 64
          },
          {
            "start": 104,
            "end": 106
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 1,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:1",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 1
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy teeth are like a flock of sheep that are even shorn, which came up from the washing; whereof every one bear twins, and none is barren among them.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 10,
            "end": 13
          },
          {
            "start": 27,
            "end": 49
          },
          {
            "start": 128,
            "end": 130
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 2,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:2",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 2
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet, and thy speech is comely: thy temples are like a piece of a pomegranate within thy locks.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 9,
            "end": 12
          },
          {
            "start": 54,
            "end": 56
          },
          {
            "start": 77,
            "end": 80
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 3,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:3",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 3
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy neck is like the tower of David builded for an armoury, whereon there hang a thousand bucklers, all shields of mighty men.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 9,
            "end": 11
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 4,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:4",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 4
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy two breasts are like two young roes that are twins, which feed among the lilies.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 16,
            "end": 19
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 5,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:5",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 5
      },
      {
        "text": "Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, I will get me to the mountain of myrrh, and to the hill of frankincense.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 6,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:6",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 6
      },
      {
        "text": "Thou art all fair, my love; there is no spot in thee.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 5,
            "end": 8
          },
          {
            "start": 28,
            "end": 36
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 7,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:7",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 7
      },
      {
        "text": "Come with me from Lebanon, my spouse, with me from Lebanon: look from the top of Amana, from the top of Shenir and Hermon, from the lions’ dens, from the mountains of the leopards.",
        "footNotes": [
          {
            "start": 30,
            "end": 36,
            "text": "Song 5:1."
          }
        ],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 27,
            "end": 29
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 8,
        "crossReferences": [
          {
            "raw": "Song 5:1.",
            "prettyString": "Song. 5:1",
            "references": [
              {
                "type": "scripture",
                "book": "songofsolomon",
                "chapters": [
                  {
                    "start": 5,
                    "end": 5,
                    "verses": [
                      {
                        "start": 1,
                        "end": 1
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:8",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 8
      },
      {
        "text": "Thou hast ravished my heart, my sister, my spouse; thou hast ravished my heart with one of thine eyes, with one chain of thy neck.",
        "footNotes": [
          {
            "start": 29,
            "end": 31,
            "text": "This form of address is an idiom to express tenderness. See also v. 12."
          },
          {
            "start": 32,
            "end": 38,
            "text": "Song 5:1."
          }
        ],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 40,
            "end": 42
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 9,
        "crossReferences": [
          {
            "raw": "Song 5:1.",
            "prettyString": "Song. 5:1",
            "references": [
              {
                "type": "scripture",
                "book": "songofsolomon",
                "chapters": [
                  {
                    "start": 5,
                    "end": 5,
                    "verses": [
                      {
                        "start": 1,
                        "end": 1
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:9",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 9
      },
      {
        "text": "How fair is thy love, my sister, my spouse! how much better is thy love than wine! and the smell of thine ointments than all spices!",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 33,
            "end": 35
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 10,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:10",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 10
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy lips, O my spouse, drop as the honeycomb: honey and milk are under thy tongue; and the smell of thy garments is like the smell of Lebanon.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 12,
            "end": 14
          },
          {
            "start": 28,
            "end": 30
          },
          {
            "start": 61,
            "end": 64
          },
          {
            "start": 113,
            "end": 115
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 11,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:11",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 11
      },
      {
        "text": "A garden inclosed is my sister, my spouse; a spring shut up, a fountain sealed.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 18,
            "end": 20
          },
          {
            "start": 32,
            "end": 34
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 12,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:12",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 12
      },
      {
        "text": "Thy plants are an orchard of pomegranates, with pleasant fruits; camphire, with spikenard,",
        "footNotes": [
          {
            "start": 65,
            "end": 73,
            "text": "OR henna."
          },
          {
            "start": 80,
            "end": 89,
            "text": "Song 1:12."
          }
        ],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 11,
            "end": 14
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 13,
        "crossReferences": [
          {
            "raw": "Song 1:12.",
            "prettyString": "Song. 1:12",
            "references": [
              {
                "type": "scripture",
                "book": "songofsolomon",
                "chapters": [
                  {
                    "start": 1,
                    "end": 1,
                    "verses": [
                      {
                        "start": 12,
                        "end": 12
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:13",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 13
      },
      {
        "text": "Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 14,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:14",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 14
      },
      {
        "text": "A fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 15,
        "crossReferences": [],
        "jstReferences": [],
        "reference": "Song of Solomon 4:15",
        "book": "Song of Solomon",
        "chapter": 4,
        "verse": 15
      },
      {
        "text": "Awake, O north wind; and come, thou south; blow upon my garden, that the spices thereof may flow out. Let my beloved come into his garden, and eat his pleasant fruits.",
        "footNotes": [],
        "italics": [
          {
            "start": 64,
            "end": 68
          }
        ],
        "associatedContent": [],
        "verseNumber": 16,
        "crossReferences": [],
